                {
                    "start_timestamp": "2026-09-10T13:04:53.673686Z",
                    "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-10T13:05:07.791226Z",
                    "flags": null,
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Nice — here's a spread of LLM/RAG project ideas, from straightforward to ambitious. Pick whatever matches the time and compute you have.\n\n**Beginner-friendly RAG projects**\n1. **Personal document Q&A bot** — Upload PDFs/notes (e.g., your own college notes), chunk + embed them, retrieve with a vector DB (FAISS/Chroma), answer via an LLM. Classic but a solid portfolio piece if you add good chunking strategy comparisons.\n2. **College resource chatbot** — RAG over your college's syllabus, timetable, and FAQ pages. Practical, demoable, and you have easy access to the source data.\n3. **Resume/JD matcher** — RAG pipeline that compares resumes against job descriptions and explains gaps, using retrieval over a skills/keywords database.\n\n**Intermediate — more novel angle**\n4. **Multi-hop RAG evaluator** — Build a RAG system, then build a second component that evaluates *how well* the retrieval worked (precision/recall of retrieved chunks vs. hallucination rate). RAG evaluation is a hot, underexplored area — good for a research-flavored project.\n5. **Hybrid search RAG** — Combine dense (embedding) retrieval with sparse (BM25/keyword) retrieval and show when each wins. Good if you want to show ML depth, not just \"call an API.\"\n6. **Domain-specific RAG with fine-tuned embeddings** — e.g., legal, medical, or code docs — fine-tune a small embedding model (or use LoRA) on domain data to beat generic embeddings on retrieval accuracy.\n7. **Conversational RAG with memory** — Add session memory so follow-up questions correctly resolve references (\"what about its side effects?\") — tests query rewriting/contextualization, a real pain point in production RAG.\n\n**Advanced / research-y**\n8. **Agentic RAG** — LLM decides *when* to retrieve, *what* to retrieve, and can issue multiple retrieval steps (like a mini ReAct agent) instead of naive single-shot retrieval — good if you want to touch agents + RAG.\n9. **RAG with structured + unstructured data fusion** — e.g., retrieving from both a SQL database and documents, letting the LLM decide which source to query (text-to-SQL + RAG combo).\n10. **Hallucination detection/mitigation pipeline** — Build a RAG system, then a secondary LLM-as-judge or NLI-based module that flags ungrounded claims in the generated answer.\n11. **Fine-tuned small LLM vs RAG comparison** — For a narrow domain, fine-tune a small open model (e.g., a 1–3B param model via LoRA) and benchmark it against a RAG pipeline on the same task — good \"compare two ML approaches\" narrative for a final-year project.\n\nA few axes to help you pick:\n- **Time you have** — weeks vs a full semester\n- **Compute access** — CPU-only vs a GPU (fine-tuning needs GPU)\n- **Goal** — resume project (pick something demoable, #1–3) vs research-flavored (pick #4, #6, #10) vs \"impress evaluators with novelty\" (#8, #9)\n\nWant me to help you scope one of these into a full proposal (problem statement, dataset, architecture, evaluation metrics)?",
