                {
                    "start_timestamp": "2026-09-10T13:06:50.032082Z",
                    "stop_timestamp": "2026-09-10T13:07:01.793632Z",
                    "flags": null,
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Here's what's actually breaking RAG systems in the real world right now, based on current industry reports:\n\n**The core problem: retrieval, not generation, is where RAG fails**\nNaive RAG pipelines fail at retrieval roughly 40% of the time — the LLM generates a confident, well-structured answer grounded in the wrong documents, and 40–60% of RAG implementations fail to reach production due to retrieval quality issues, governance gaps, and the inability to explain decisions to regulators. That's a real, high-stakes gap — most tutorials never touch it.\n\n**Pick one of these — each is a genuine unsolved pain point, not a toy:**\n\n1. **Silent retrieval failure detection.** Sometimes the right document exists in the database and retrieval simply never returns it — the model never sees the information, and this \"silent failure\" happens quietly with no error thrown. Build a system that *detects* when retrieval likely failed (before generation even happens) rather than just answering confidently on wrong context. This is a genuinely open problem in production RAG.\n\n2. **Stale/outdated knowledge base handling.** Without proper document versioning, systems end up with multiple versions of the same content creating contradictory answers, confidently retrieving two different versions of the same policy and leaving users to guess which is current. Build a RAG pipeline that tracks document freshness/versioning and actively resolves conflicting retrieved chunks instead of blindly feeding both to the LLM.\n\n3. **Incremental re-indexing without downtime.** Batch pipelines that deprecate old content often leave orphaned vectors in the index for days, so users get search results pointing to documents that no longer exist — this needs explicit deletion tracking, not just additive syncing. Build a live-updating RAG system (not nightly batch) that handles adds/edits/deletes correctly and measurably improves freshness SLAs.\n\n4. **Cross-chunk reasoning failure.** Long-context vs RAG is one of the biggest architecture debates of 2026 partly because RAG struggles when the answer doesn't exist in any single chunk — i.e., the answer is scattered across multiple documents/sections. Build a retrieval system that explicitly detects this and does multi-chunk synthesis (graph-RAG or multi-hop retrieval) instead of top-k stuffing.\n\n5. **RAG evaluation/drift monitoring.** The retrieval that worked on day one degrades silently as the corpus grows — evaluation drift is the second biggest cause of poor RAG quality after bad retrieval itself. Build a continuous evaluation harness that catches retrieval quality regression automatically as a knowledge base scales, rather than a one-time benchmark.\n\n**My honest pick for a final-year project:** #1 (silent failure detection) or #5 (drift monitoring) — both are things real companies are struggling with *today*, they're novel enough to defend in a viva, and they don't require massive compute (no fine-tuning needed, mostly clever retrieval + eval engineering).\n\nWant me to scope one of these into a full proposal — architecture, dataset, evaluation metrics, and a realistic timeline?",
